From afee989f8f194fe7def2fcb5e7b8322fe1fd68b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilan Joselevich <personal@ilanjoselevich.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 19:22:36 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 05/12] scheduler: serve its state and every change as
 Events.Watch

A dashboard that draws a farm live, or anything else that follows the
scheduler, needs its state and every change to it. The metrics are
sampled and name no derivations.

The Dispatcher now keeps a few facts it had no use for before: when an
entry was placed, whether the cache was asked about it, where its
inputs come from, and each Schedule caller's name and connect time.
pick() scores the same workers in the same order.

It builds events: the connected workers and clients, every queued or
assigned derivation, and what happened to each (wanted, assigned,
built, failed, answered from the cache, given back, requeued, dropped).
Events say what happened, since a diff of the state loses outcomes.
Dispatcher::watch() gets a snapshot, then one event per change, both
built under the Dispatcher's mutex, so the first change carries the
snapshot's seq plus one. It builds events only while someone watches.
With nobody watching, dispatcher-bench firehose spends about 2% more
time in the Dispatcher.

The messages live in their own proto/events.proto, so clients vendor
one file and nix_remote.proto stays untouched. The Events service sends
them on one server stream, Watch. Only the active scheduler serves it,
and it ends open streams with UNAVAILABLE when it steps down. It
cancels a watcher that falls behind, with a bound that grows with the
largest table the stream has seen. Watch needs the watch role and turns
away anonymous callers, since the stream names clients and derivations.
It allows 64 streams, 8 per caller.

tests/events-test.cc runs a seeded random farm against one Dispatcher
while a watcher rebuilds the state from the events. After every step
that state must equal a fresh snapshot. events-grpc-test runs the
service with certificates and bearer tokens.
